Toward an Adaptive Resolution of the Blockchain Trilemma

A Formally Verified, Safe, and Energy-Aware RL-Driven Consensus Framework

Version 4


ouadi Maakoul 


Abstract.  

We formalise the blockchain trilemma as a multi‑objective
optimisation over continuously measurable scalability, security,
decentralisation, and energy metrics.  A stochastic game with five
parameterised attack families captures the adversarial environment.
The consensus policy is trained via shielded reinforcement learning,
where a safety shield and a separate liveness monitor enforce
correctness invariants, and a trust‑aware replay buffer using an
Isolation Forest detector aims to mitigate time‑delayed poisoning.
Factored shield synthesis promises scalability.  A weighted‑Chebyshev
scalarisation alternative allows recovery of non‑convex regions of
the Pareto front.  Under an assumption of diminishing shield
interference—whose achievability under an adaptive adversary remains
an open problem—the shielded policy can be shown to converge to a
local Pareto‑optimal point on the emergent energy‑aware trilemma
frontier.  An interruptibility operator enables live protocol
migration while attempting to preserve user‑specified bounds on all
three trilemma dimensions, with the exactness of the required shadow
simulation identified as a critical unsolved challenge.  The entire
design is algorithmically specified and accompanied by a formal
verification roadmap; we report on the current state of machine‑checked
proofs for the underlying consensus core and outline the remaining
gaps.

---

1. SYSTEM MODEL AND OPERATIONAL TRILEMMA METRICS

Definition 1.1 (Blockchain System).  A blockchain system is a tuple

 B = (N, T, C, S, E)

where
 N = {1, …, N}  set of nodes,
 T         transaction space,
 C         consensus mechanism (function from local views to decisions),
 S         global state space,
 E : S → R_{≥0}  energy consumption (cumulative joules per epoch).

Evolution:
 s_{t+1} = F(s_t, a_t, ξ_t)

with a_t ∈ A (consensus action) and ξ_t ∼ P(·|s_t) covering network
delays and adversarial perturbations.

---

1.1  Operational Trilemma Metrics

We give protocol‑agnostic functions on S, all mapping to [0,1].

Scalability
 S(s) = τ(s) / τ_max
 τ(s) = confirmed tx/s; τ_max is a hardware‑bound throughput limit.

Security
 Q(s) = 1 – Φ(s) / Φ₀
 Φ(s) = minimal fraction of honest stake (or resources) an adversary
 must corrupt to break safety or liveness.
 Φ₀ = nominal fault tolerance (e.g., 1/2 for BFT, 1/2 for longest‑chain
 PoS under synchrony).

For BFT‑class protocols, Φ(s) is computable in closed form:
Φ(s) = (N – f)/N, where f is the number of Byzantine nodes tolerated.
To make Q falsifiable across broader protocol families, we provide
coarse but explicit under‑approximations Φ̂(s) ≤ Φ(s) for two non‑BFT
settings:

Nakamoto PoS (longest‑chain):  Let the set of validators and their
stakes be known.  Define the minimum stake required to cause a
persistent re‑organisation from depth k as the smallest total stake
that could build a heavier alternative chain, given the observed
fork‑rate and block‑arrival process.  Following the deepest‑common‑
ancestor analysis, we estimate
 Φ̂_Nakamoto(s) = (stake needed to overtake the canonical chain’s
 deepest confirmed block) / (total active stake).
Even when exact reorg‑cost cannot be computed on‑chain, a safe bound
can be derived from the Nakamoto coefficient of the stake distribution.

Avalanche (Snowball):  Let k be the sample size and α the quorum
threshold (typical defaults k = 20, α = 15).  The protocol resists
Byzantine behaviours while the adversary controls less than
α – k/2 of the total weight.  Hence we set
 Φ̂_Avalanche(s) = max(0, (k·α – k/2) / total weight).
This is a straight‑line approximation of the security margin; precise
parameter‑dependent guarantees are available from the Snowball
analysis.

For any other consensus family we require a similarly safe
under‑approximation, computable from protocol state.  When no
closed form exists, one may fall back to the generic estimator
Φ̂_generic(s) = 1 – (observed adversarial sway) / (total sway),
calibrated by a monitoring oracle.

Decentralisation
 D(s) = H(s) / H_max
 H(s) = Shannon entropy of block‑production shares [Zhang2022],
 H_max = log₂ N.

Energy
 ε(s) = E(s) / E_max
 E(s) captures compute and network consumption.  To avoid masking
 energy‑intensive validators, we use the 95th percentile of the
 validator energy distribution rather than the median.  If the
 variance of validator energy use exceeds twice the mean, governance
 is advised to rescale the energy weight λ_E.  The model uses a
 linear combination E(s) = c_cpu · T_cpu + c_net · bytes_sent, with
 the constants calibrated offline.  The 95th‑percentile choice is a
 robustness measure; a full sensitivity analysis is left for future
 empirical work (cf. [Platt2022], which documents hardware‑dependent
 variability but does not provide an error bound for this linear
 model).

All four metrics are continuously computable on S.

