1. ADVERSARIAL MODEL: STOCHASTIC GAME WITH FIVE ATTACK FAMILIES

Nodes are partitioned into honest N_H and adversarial N_A,
|N_A|/N ≤ β < 1/2.  Each agent runs a policy π_i : S → Δ(A_i).
The joint action a_t = (a^H_t, a^A_t) determines the next state.

The adversary chooses its attack from one of five families,
each operating under a bounded budget:

 A1. Naive Malicious – stateless misbehaviour (equivocation, invalid
 blocks).

 A2. Collusive Rumor – coordinated dissemination of false blocks or
 votes, exploiting gossip topology.

 A3. Adaptive Adversarial – observes π_H and optimises a^A_t online,
 possibly using its own learned model of honest behaviour.

 A4. Byzantine Fault Injection – state‑correlated faults in up to
 f nodes, respecting the fault threshold.

 A5. Time‑Delayed Poisoning – injects training data that appears
 benign initially but becomes harmful after a long delay (poisons the
 RL replay buffer).

The adversary’s objective is to minimise E[Q(s_t)] subject to its
budget.

