1. PARETO FRONTIER AND ASYMPTOTIC CONVERGENCE

Definition 4.1 (Feasible Region).  For the policy class Π (including
shield σ_s and monitor μ), the feasible region is

 F = { (S(s), Q(s), D(s), ε(s)) ∈ R^4 ∣ s reachable under some π ∈ Π }.

The Pareto frontier P ⊂ F consists of all points not strictly
dominated in all four dimensions.

Assumption 4.2 (Ergodicity under Shielded Policy).  For every θ,
the Markov chain induced by the shielded policy and the liveness monitor
is irreducible and aperiodic on a communicating class S_reset.
Transient states are absorbed by a recovery routine that resets to a
safe state.  (While we have not formally verified this assumption for
the full composite system, it is a standard requirement for policy
gradient convergence.)

Theorem 4.3 (Conditional Local Pareto Improvement under Shielded PPO).
Assume J(θ) is continuously differentiable.  If the shield σ_s
intervenes with probability p_int(θ) and there exists a training
regime in which p_int(θ) → 0 (for example, through regularisation
that penalises shield interventions), then PPO updates with
sufficiently small step size and unbiased advantage estimates (on the
shielded trajectories) converge a.s. to a stationary point θ^* of J.
At θ^*, the four objectives satisfy first‑order Pareto optimality
conditions.

We stress that the assumption p_int(θ) → 0 is not guaranteed by the
current framework.  An adaptive adversary (A3) could actively drive
the system to safety boundaries, keeping intervention probability
non‑negligible.  Obtaining convergence guarantees in that regime is
an open problem; Theorem 4.3 is therefore best viewed as a
characterisation of the idealised scenario where the shield becomes
progressively unnecessary.  An empirical investigation of shield
intervention rates under A1, A2 and A3 is a high‑priority item for
future simulation studies.

Corollary 4.4 (Frontier Tracing via Scalarisation).  By sweeping
λ_S, λ_Q, λ_D, λ_E, the algorithm traces the Pareto frontier.
However, linear scalarisation can only recover solutions on the convex
hull of the frontier.  To reach non‑convex regions we adopt the
weighted Chebyshev scalarisation (as in the MOCHA algorithm
[Hairi2025]),

 r_t^cheb = – max_i w_i ( z_i^* – f_i(s_t) ),

where f_1 = S, f_2 = Q, f_3 = D, f_4 = –ε (so that all four objectives
are maximised) and z_i^* are ideal points.  The Chebyshev‑based variant
is compatible with the shielded PPO loop and, under the same
intervention‑diminishing condition, can recover points on the full
Pareto front.

Empirical status.  A recent study by Gutierrez et al. (2025) applied
unshielded PPO to adapt consensus parameters and reported a 34 %
latency reduction and up to 17 % energy savings in crash‑fault
settings.  This provides partial evidence that RL can improve
consensus performance.  However, those experiments did not include a
safety shield, an adaptive adversary, or the energy metrics proposed
here.  Construction of a simulation testbed that systematically
evaluates our shielded framework under all five attack families is
underway; an illustrative Pareto‑front plot derived from a simplified
analytical model will be presented in the extended version.

