1. INTERRUPTIBILITY OPERATOR WITH TRILEMMA‑AWARE INVARIANTS

Definition 5.1 (Interruptibility Operator).
I : S × A × P → S × A, with P a protocol descriptor (hash of new
consensus module).  The transition is (s', a') = I(s, a, p).

The operator aims to preserve user‑specified bounds:

 Q(s') ≥ Q_min, D(s') ≥ D_min, S(s') ≤ S_max,
 ε(s') ≤ ε(s) (1 + δ).

The thresholds are set by governance.

Algorithm 2: safe_interrupt(s_t, p_new)

```
1.  verify that p_new carries valid machine‑checked proofs of φ_safety, φ_liveness
2.  pre_snapshot ← (S(s), Q(s), D(s), ε(s))
3.  simulate transition to p_new on a shadow state → s_sim
4.  metrics_sim ← (S(s_sim), Q(s_sim), D(s_sim), ε(s_sim))
5.  if Q(s_sim) ≥ Q_min and D(s_sim) ≥ D_min and S(s_sim) ≤ S_max
        and ε(s_sim) ≤ ε(s)(1+δ) then
6.       broadcast reconfig proposal to validators
7.       wait for supermajority (≥ 2f+1 signatures)
8.       if collected, atomically migrate state; return s_sim
9.  else return REJECT
```

Critical limitation.  The correctness of Algorithm 2 depends
entirely on the assumption that the shadow simulation s_sim exactly
matches the state that will be reached after migration.  In a
distributed, adversarial environment, guaranteeing such an exact
replica is a major unsolved problem (cf. ADACON [Anon2024], which
identifies secure transition protocols as future work).  A more robust
design would incorporate a rollback mechanism, reverting to a
checkpoint if the post‑migration state deviates beyond a threshold.
Formalising and proving the safety of such a rollback protocol is
future work.  Consequently, the earlier formulation as a theorem is
retracted; we present only the algorithm and state the open problem.

