1. RELATED WORK

We position our contribution with respect to several lines of work.

Trilemma formalism.  Nakai et al. (2023) proposed a constant‑product
formula for the PoW trilemma.  Mssassi & Abou El Kalam (2024) attempted
a formal proof of the trilemma; this claim is contested in a formal
rebuttal by Wright (2025), who argues that such proofs require
operationally defined metrics.  Our approach directly addresses that
critique: we construct concrete, computable metrics S, Q, D, ε that
allow the trilemma to be measured rather than assumed.

RL‑based consensus.  Gutierrez et al. (2025) used graph‑based PPO
to adapt consensus parameters, achieving 34 % latency reduction and
up to 16 % energy savings.  ADACON [Anon2024] employs Bayesian
optimisation to switch among PoW, PoS, PBFT, PoA, and DPoS, reaching
29.7 ms latency in a 1 000‑node simulation.  Our work extends these
lines by adding formal safety/liveness shields, a richer adversarial
taxonomy, multi‑objective energy‑aware optimisation, and a
verification roadmap—distinguishing itself from purely heuristic or
single‑objective approaches.

Shielded safe RL.  Alshiekh et al. (2017) introduced safety shields
for single‑agent RL.  Compositional shielding for multi‑agent systems
was recently formalised by Brorholt et al. (2025).  Georgescu et al.
(2025) addressed liveness preservation under GR(1) specifications.
We combine these techniques in a blockchain consensus setting, with a
factorisation that differs from Brorholt et al. by operating over
action dimensions rather than agents.

Formal BFT verification.  Carr et al. (2022) mechanised HotStuff’s
safety proof in Agda.  We adopt Isabelle/HOL for consistency with the
broader verification ecosystem, and our verification plan builds on
the reusable invariants they identified.

