1. CONCLUSION AND FORMAL CLAIMS

Claim 1 (Measurable Trilemma).  The operational metrics
S, Q, D, ε map the trilemma into a 4‑dimensional feasible region
without a hard multiplicative bound.  The frontier emerges from system
constraints.  Closed‑form under‑approximations for Nakamoto PoS and
Avalanche make Q falsifiable; analogous approximations must be
developed for other protocol families as needed.

Claim 2 (Safe Adaptivity).  The safety shield σ_s guarantees
□ φ_safety for any learning updates of π_θ.  For liveness, the
system inherits the base protocol’s □◇ new_block_confirmed guarantee
under fault bound β; a fully mechanised proof of composite liveness
remains future work.

Claim 3 (Conditional Convergence).  Under the assumption that
shield interventions become negligible (not proved achievable under
adaptive adversaries), PPO with shielding can converge to a local
Pareto optimum.  With Chebyshev scalarisation the algorithm can cover
non‑convex regions of the frontier.  Empirical validation is ongoing.

Claim 4 (Interruptible Evolution).  The interruptibility operator
is designed to preserve user‑specified trilemma bounds when the shadow
simulation is exact.  Achieving provable correctness with a realistic
rollback protocol is an open problem; the current algorithm is a
blueprint.

Dependency graph:

· Assumption 4.2 (ergodicity) + shield non‑interference → Theorem 4.3 (conditional PPO convergence) → Corollary 4.4 (frontier tracing).
· Base protocol’s liveness proof + monitor μ → liveness of adaptive system (informally).
· Exact shadow simulation → correctness of Algorithm 2 (open problem).

