Appendix A — Mathematical Background and Formal Definitions
================================================================

A.1  System Model and Transition Semantics

We model the blockchain as a discrete‑time stochastic dynamical system
over an infinite horizon.  The state space S is assumed to be a
compact metric space that includes all values relevant for consensus
(ledger forks, validator sets, stake distributions, recent block times,
pending transaction pools).  The action space A is finite
(see §D.1).  The transition function

s_{t+1} = F(s_t, a_t, \xi_t)

is measurable, where \xi_t is a random variable drawn from a
distribution P(\cdot \mid s_t) that captures network latency,
message drops, and adversarial perturbations.  For a given honest
policy \pi_H and adversary policy \pi_A, the joint policy
\pi = (\pi_H, \pi_A) induces a probability measure over trajectories
\tau = (s_0, a_0, s_1, a_1, \ldots).  All expectations \mathbb{E}
are taken with respect to this measure unless otherwise noted.

A.2  Operational Metrics — Detailed Construction

Scalability S(s): Let \tau(s) be the average number of
transactions confirmed per second over a sliding window of W blocks,
and \tau_{\max} the maximum throughput achievable on the reference
hardware.  Then S(s) = \min(\tau(s)/\tau_{\max}, 1).  The window
length is chosen long enough to smooth short‑term variance.

Security Q(s): The nominal fault tolerance \Phi_0 is protocol‑
specific.  For BFT protocols we set \Phi_0 = \frac{1}{2} (or the
protocol’s proven bound).  For a given state s, we estimate the
actual required corruption fraction \Phi(s) as an under‑approximation
\widehat{\Phi}(s):

· BFT class: \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{BFT}}(s) = (N - f)/N, where
  f is the observed number of Byzantine nodes tolerated in the
  current configuration.
· Nakamoto PoS: Let v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_n be validator stakes,
  sorted descending.  The Nakamoto coefficient k_N is the minimum
  number of validators that together control more than one half of the
  total stake.  A reorganisation from depth d requires an adversary
  to build an alternative chain heavier than the honest chain; a
  necessary condition is that the adversary holds more stake than the
  largest d honest validators combined.  We set
  \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Nakamoto}}(s) = \frac{1}{2} \cdot
    \frac{\text{stake of the smallest set controlling } \ge \frac12 \text{ total stake}}{\text{total stake}}.
  A more precise bound using the deepest‑common‑ancestor analysis
  yields \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Nakamoto}}(s) = 
    \frac{\text{min stake to outweigh } \lceil \frac{k}{2} \rceil \text{ consecutive confirmations}}{\text{total active stake}}
  where k is the confirmation depth; this can be computed from the
  current fork tree.
· Avalanche (Snowball): The protocol’s safety proof assumes the
  adversary controls at most  \alpha - k/2  of the total weight.
  Hence we set \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Avalanche}}(s) = 
    \max(0, \frac{k\alpha - k/2}{\text{total weight}}), with k=20,
    \alpha=15 as default parameters.  This is a linear bound that
  reasonably well approximates the actual security margin when the
  adversary is bounded.
· Generic fallback: If no analytic formula exists, we maintain a
  monitoring oracle that estimates the adversary’s effective sway
  \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{gen}}(s) = 1 - \frac{\text{honest sway}}{\text{total sway}},
  where “sway” is a protocol‑dependent resource (stake, computation,
  storage).  This oracle must be conservative.

The security metric is then Q(s) = 1 - \widehat{\Phi}(s)/\Phi_0,
clamped to [0,1].

Decentralisation D(s): Let p_i(s) be the fraction of blocks
produced by validator i over the last W blocks.
H(s) = -\sum_{i=1}^N p_i(s) \log_2 p_i(s), with the convention
0 \log_2 0 = 0.  The maximum possible entropy is \log_2 N;
thus D(s) = H(s)/\log_2 N.  This metric captures both the number
of active validators and the uniformity of their block production.

Energy \varepsilon(s): We define the energy consumption per epoch
as

E(s) = c_{\text{cpu}} \cdot T_{\text{cpu}}(s) + c_{\text{net}} \cdot \text{bytes\_sent}(s),

where T_{\text{cpu}}(s) is the total CPU time consumed by consensus
operations (validating, voting, leader election) during the epoch, and
\text{bytes\_sent}(s) is the total network traffic generated.
The constants c_{\text{cpu}}, c_{\text{net}} (joules per CPU‑second
and joules per byte) are calibrated offline.  To account for
heterogeneous hardware, we compute E(s) for each validator and then
take the 95th percentile as the system‑wide energy figure, rather
than the median.  This ensures that the energy measure is sensitive to
the most energy‑intensive nodes.  If the variance of validator energy
use exceeds twice the mean, governance should rescale \lambda_E.
The metric is then \varepsilon(s) = E(s)/E_{\max}, where E_{\max}
is the maximum permissible energy per epoch.

A.3  Stochastic Game Formulation

The system is modelled as a partially observable stochastic game
(POSG) with N agents.  Honest agents share the policy \pi_\theta;
the adversary is a single controller of up to f nodes.  The game
is zero‑sum in the security dimension but general‑sum overall because
the honest agents also value scalability, decentralisation, and low
energy.

Honest objective: \max_{\pi_\theta} J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\pi_A}[\sum_t \gamma^t r_t].

Adversary objective: \min_{\pi_A} \mathbb{E}[Q(s_t)], subject to
budget constraints  \sum_t \text{cost}(a^A_t) \le B.

The adversary’s action space is partitioned into five families
(A1‑A5).  Each family defines a sub‑policy that can be activated.

A.4  Shield and Liveness Monitor Formal Specifications

Safety Shield: Given an LTL formula \varphi_{\text{safety}} and a
discrete abstraction \mathcal{M} of the system’s state machine, the
shield \sigma_s : S \times A \to A \cup \{\text{reject}\} is
constructed by reactive synthesis as the maximal non‑blocking winning
strategy from the safe states.  Formally, let
\mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} \subseteq S be the maximal set of states
from which the base protocol guarantees \varphi_{\text{safety}}
indefinitely under any sequence of honest actions.  For any state
s \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} and action a \in A, we define
\sigma_s(s,a) = a if
F(s,a,\xi) \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} for all \xi in the
support of P(\cdot|s) (for the abstraction, this condition is
checked on the discrete grid).  Otherwise, \sigma_s(s,a) = \text{reject}.
When \sigma_s rejects, the system falls back to a statically
verified safe default a_{\text{safe}}(s).

The factoring into sub‑shields is possible because
\varphi_{\text{safety}} is a conjunction of independent properties:
block validity (depends on block_size and timeout_ratio) and
commit quorum intersection (depends on commit_rule).  The sub‑shields
are synthesised over smaller alphabets and their conjunction trivially
preserves the global property.

Liveness Monitor: The monitor \mu : S \times \mathbb{N} \to \{0,1\}
maintains a round counter \rho that increments with each consensus
step.  If the system has not produced a new confirmed block within
k steps, \mu triggers and forces a_{\text{safe}}(s).  The
constant k is chosen such that under the base protocol’s liveness
assumptions (bounded message delay, eventually synchronous periods),
a_{\text{safe}} eventually leads to confirmation.

A.5  Pareto Optimality and Scalarisation

A policy \pi induces a long‑term average (or discounted) vector
\mathbf{J}(\pi) = (S^\pi, Q^\pi, D^\pi, -\varepsilon^\pi) where we
negate energy so that all objectives are maximised.  Let
\mathcal{F} be the feasible region of these vectors for policies in
\Pi (shielded policies).  A point \mathbf{J}^* \in \mathcal{F} is
Pareto optimal if there is no other \mathbf{J} \in \mathcal{F} such
that J_i \ge J_i^* for all i and strict inequality for at least
one dimension.

Linear scalarisation optimises a weighted sum
\sum_{i=1}^4 w_i J_i with w_i > 0.  This yields solutions on the
convex hull of \mathcal{F}.  Weighted Chebyshev scalarisation
minimises \max_i w_i (z_i^* - J_i), where z_i^* are ideal points
(an upper bound for each objective).  For appropriate choices of w_i,
the Chebyshev norm can recover any Pareto‑optimal point, including
those in non‑convex regions, because the level sets of the Chebyshev
metric are non‑convex and can “wedge” into concavities of the frontier
[VanMoffaert2013].

Appendix B — Proofs
=========================

B.1  Shield Safety Proof (Sketch)

Lemma B.1 (Perfect Safety Shield): Let \sigma_s be synthesised as
described.  For any state s \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}} and any
action a such that \sigma_s(s,a) \ne \text{reject}, the successor
state s' under any \xi satisfies s' \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}.

Proof. The synthesis procedure guarantees that the shield disables all
transitions leaving \mathcal{S}_{\text{safe}}.  The concrete shield
automaton is the winning strategy of the safety game; by correctness of
reactive synthesis (e.g., Strix/Ltlsynt), every allowed action keeps
the abstract state within the winning region.  The discretisation
introduces an error bounded by \epsilon(\delta) (Conjecture 3.4),
but if \delta is sufficiently small, \epsilon(\delta) < \text{margin}
between safe and unsafe regions, then the abstraction is conservative
and the shield remains safe in the original system.  ∎

Lemma B.2 (Shield Composition): Suppose \varphi_1, \varphi_2 are
LTL properties over disjoint action variables, and \varphi = \varphi_1 \land \varphi_2.
If \sigma_1 is a shield for \varphi_1 and \sigma_2 for \varphi_2,
then the product shield (\sigma_1,\sigma_2) (applied sequentially,
where rejection by either leads to rejection) is a shield for \varphi.

Proof. Safety properties are preserved under conjunction.  Because the
action variables are independent, an action safe for \varphi_1 and
\varphi_2 individually is safe for their conjunction.  ∎

B.2  Liveness Inheritance

The monitor \mu overrides the agent with the safe default a_{\text{safe}}(s)
after at most k idle rounds.  By the assumed liveness of the base
protocol, from any state, repeatedly applying a_{\text{safe}} guarantees
a new block eventually.  Thus, even if the RL agent proposes actions
that the shield rejects or that do not produce blocks, the monitor
ensures that progress is made within bounded time.  This provides
\Box\Diamond \text{new\_block\_confirmed} under the base protocol’s
liveness assumptions.

B.3  Proof of Theorem 4.3 (Conditional Local Convergence)

We restate the theorem:

Theorem 4.3. If J(\theta) is continuously differentiable, and the
shield intervention probability p_{\text{int}}(\theta) \to 0 as
training progresses, then PPO with unbiased advantage estimates on
the shielded trajectories converges almost surely to a stationary point
\theta^* of J.  At \theta^*, the weighted sum of the four
objectives satisfies first‑order Pareto stationarity.

Proof sketch. The shielded PPO objective is

L(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{(s,a)\sim \pi_{\text{shielded}}} \left[ \min\left( \frac{\pi_\theta(a|s)}{\pi_{\text{old}}(a|s)} \hat{A}, \text{clip}\right) \right].

Because the safety shield may replace the raw action a_{\text{raw}}
with a different action a, the effective policy is
\tilde{\pi}_\theta(a|s) = \sum_{a'} \pi_\theta(a'|s) \cdot \mathbf{1}\{\sigma_s(s,a') = a\}.
When p_{\text{int}} \to 0, \tilde{\pi}_\theta \to \pi_\theta
pointwise for all states (except a set of measure zero where the shield
intervenes).  Under such limiting equivalence, the gradient of the
expected return with respect to \theta approaches the gradient of the
unshielded objective J(\theta).  PPO is a variant of natural policy
gradient; under standard assumptions (smoothness, bounded rewards,
appropriate step size schedule) it converges to a stationary point
[Alshiekh2017, Schulman2017].  Since J(\theta) is a weighted sum,
stationarity implies that no local direction simultaneously improves
all four weighted objectives; this is exactly the first‑order necessary
condition for local Pareto optimality (the Karush‑Kuhn‑Tucker conditions
for the multi‑objective problem).  ∎

The assumption that p_{\text{int}} \to 0 is essential to avoid
gradient bias.  Without it, convergence may be to a stationary point
of the shielded objective, which may differ from the true trilemma
trade‑offs.

B.4  Chebyshev Scalarisation Stationarity

When replacing the linear reward with the Chebyshev scalarised reward
r_t^{\text{cheb}} = -\max_i w_i (z_i^* - f_i(s_t)), the objective
J_{\text{cheb}}(\theta) becomes non‑linear but Lipschitz.  Under the
same convergence conditions, PPO converges to a stationary point of
this scalarised objective.  Because Chebyshev scalarisation is a
complete scalarisation method (any Pareto‑optimal point can be recovered
by suitable weights and ideal points), the set of stationary points
covers the Pareto front.  The proof of completeness for Chebyshev norms
in multi‑objective RL is given in [Hairi2025].

Appendix C — Architecture and Design Details
=================================================

C.1  Overall System Architecture

The system consists of:

· Validators: each runs a local copy of the shielded actor \pi_\theta,
  the safety shield \sigma_s, and the liveness monitor \mu.
· Centralised Critic (Training): during off‑chain training epochs, a
  critic network V_\phi(s) is maintained in a secure enclave and
  updated using global trajectories.
· Shield Synthesis Module: an offline toolchain that takes the LTL
  specification and the transition abstraction and generates the safety
  automaton \sigma_s (in C/C++/Rust) that is embedded in the validator
  client.
· Monitoring and Alerting: the Isolation Forest anomaly detector
  continuously scores transitions and tags samples in the replay buffer.
· Governance Contract: stores the trilemma bounds Q_{\min}, D_{\min}, S_{\max}
  and the weight vector \lambda; only it can trigger the interruptibility
  operator.

Fig. C1 (not shown) would illustrate the data flow from validators to
the replay buffer, shield filtering, and the critic update loop.

C.2  Safety Shield Synthesis Pipeline

1. Model extraction: The base consensus protocol’s state machine is
   expressed in a formal language (e.g., SMV or TLA+).  The LTL property
   \varphi_{\text{safety}} is also given.
2. Abstraction: Continuous variables (S, Q, D) are discretised into
   finite intervals of width \delta.  The transition relation is
   over‑approximated to preserve safety: if s \to s' is possible in
   the continuous system, then the abstract transition exists.
3. Game construction: The interaction between the shield (controller)
   and the environment (adversary + network) is modelled as a two‑player
   safety game.  The shield’s moves are the allowed actions; the
   environment’s moves are the nondeterministic outcomes.
4. Synthesis: A tool like Strix solves the game and produces a
   Mealy machine that, given current abstract state and proposed action,
   outputs either the same action or “reject”.  This machine is then
   converted into an efficient lookup table.

The error introduced by discretisation is conjectured to satisfy
\epsilon(\delta) \le L_F \cdot \delta \cdot T, where L_F is the
Lipschitz constant of the transition function over the horizon T.
Since a formal bound for L_F in HotStuff‑like protocols is not yet
available, we choose \delta based on a worst‑case sensitivity analysis
from simulation (future work).

C.3  Liveness Monitor Design

The monitor maintains a counter stale_rounds.  Every time a new block
is confirmed, the counter resets to zero.  If the counter reaches
max_stale = k, the monitor enters an override state.  In the override
mode, the monitor ignores the actor’s output and repeatedly proposes
a_{\text{safe}} until a new block is confirmed.  The safe default
action is protocol‑specific; for HotStuff it is a standard round‑robin
leader proposal with conservative timeout.  The constant k is set
to a value larger than the expected worst‑case confirmation time under
the fault model (e.g., k = 10 epochs when the network is
synchronous and f faults can delay progress by at most 5 rounds).

C.4  Trust‑Aware Replay Buffer with Isolation Forest

The anomaly detector is an Isolation Forest (IF) [Liu2008] trained on
normal protocol trajectories.  The feature vector consists of:

· block_interval: time since previous block,
· mempool_size: number of unconfirmed transactions,
· peer_count: number of connected peers,
· vote_convergence: fraction of nodes converging on the same block
  after one round.
  These features capture basic health metrics without requiring deep
  inspection of block content.  The IF assigns an anomaly score
  s \in [0,1]; lower scores indicate normal behaviour.  We set a
  threshold \tau_{\min} = 0.7.  Any transition with score above
  \tau_{\min} is considered suspicious and removed from the replay
  buffer before sampling.  The IF is retrained every 1,000 epochs using
  the entire buffer of accepted transitions (which are presumed clean
  after the initial filtering).  This retraining adapts to slowly
  drifting protocol behaviour.  Note: this is a heuristic; a poisoning
  attacker could craft subtle attacks that evade the IF.  The defence is
  not provably secure.

C.5  CTDE and MPC Aggregation

During training, validators share their trajectory data with a central
training coordinator.  To avoid a single trusted party, we propose a
small committee of 5 foundation nodes that perform secure multi‑party
computation (MPC) for gradient aggregation.  Specifically, each
validator encrypts its local gradient using an additive secret‑sharing
scheme; the 5 nodes compute the sum of gradients using a 3‑out‑of‑5
threshold protocol (tolerating up to 2 malicious aggregators).  The
aggregated gradient is then public and used to update the global
critic.  The actors are trained locally using policy gradients
transmitted from the coordinator.  After training, the critic is
discarded and only the actor weights are deployed on validators.

C.6  Interruptibility Protocol and Rollback Extension

Algorithm 2 (safe_interrupt) relies on an exact shadow simulation.
To strengthen this, we sketch a rollback‑enhanced version:

After step 8, instead of immediately finalising, the system enters a
trial period of T_{\text{trial}} epochs under the new protocol,
during which the old protocol’s state is kept as a checkpoint.  A
monitoring oracle compares the actual metrics (S', Q', D', \varepsilon')
with the simulated ones.  If the deviation exceeds a threshold
\Delta_{\text{tol}}, an automatic rollback to the checkpoint is
initiated by a supermajority vote.  Formalising the safety and
liveness of this rollback requires a proof that the checkpoint can
always be re‑established; this is left open.

Appendix D — Technical Specifications and Algorithms
=========================================================

D.1  Action Space Encoding

The action space comprises four discrete dimensions:

· block_size: {1KB, 10KB, 100KB, 1MB} → encoded as integer 0‑3,
· timeout_ratio: {0.5, 1.0, 2.0} × base_timeout → 0‑2,
· commit_rule: {sync_threshold, async_quorum} → 0,1,
· leader_schedule: {VRand, weighted_VRF} → 0,1 (round_robin is
  excluded because it can be unsafe under adaptive adversary; the
  shield restricts it).

Thus the raw action is a tuple (i_1, i_2, i_3, i_4), 96 possible
combinations.  The policy network outputs logits for each dimension
independently (a factored policy).

D.2  Energy Model Calibration

Offline, a reference implementation is profiled on representative
hardware (e.g., AWS c5.xlarge for CPU, 10 Gbps network).  We measure:

c_{\text{cpu}} = \frac{\text{energy consumption of a signing+verification cycle}}{\text{CPU time of that cycle}},\quad
c_{\text{net}} = \frac{\text{energy per bit sent}}{\text{avg message size}}.

The total energy per epoch for validator i is
E_i = c_{\text{cpu}} T_{\text{cpu},i} + c_{\text{net}} B_i.
The system‑wide energy is
E(s) = \text{95th percentile of } \{E_i\}.
E_{\max} is set to the energy of a worst‑case epoch under the
current maximum load.

D.3  Full Algorithm Pseudocode

Algorithm D.3: Safe‑RL Consensus Loop (detailed)

```
Input: initial θ, φ, W (rolling window), L (shield interpolation tables)
Output: trained θ*

1:  σ_s ← load_shield(L)                  // pre‑synthesised
2:  μ_counter ← 0
3:  B ← TrustAwareReplayBuffer(capacity=1e6, threshold τ_min)
4:  IF_detector ← IsolationForest()     // initial model
5:
6:  for episode = 1 to M do
7:      s ← genesis_state()
8:      for t = 1 to T do
9:          a_raw ∼ π_θ(·|s)
10:         a ← σ_s.apply(s, a_raw)
11:         if a = REJECT then
12:             a ← a_safe(s)
13:         end if
14:         if μ_counter ≥ k then
15:             a ← a_safe(s)
16:             μ_counter ← 0
17:         end if
18:         s’ ← step(s, a)
19:         if new_block_confirmed(s’) then
20:             μ_counter ← 0
21:         else
22:             μ_counter ← μ_counter + 1
23:         end if
24:         r ← compute_reward(s, a, s’)   // linear or Chebyshev
25:         trust_score ← IF_detector.score([block_interval,
26:                             mempool_size, peer_count])
27:         B.push((s, a, r, s’, trust_score))
28:         s ← s’
29:
30:         if B.size > batch_size then
31:             minibatch ← B.sample_trusted(τ_min, batch_size)
32:             advantages ← GAE(minibatch, V_ϕ)
33:             θ ← PPO_update(θ, minibatch, advantages)
34:             ϕ ← critic_update(ϕ, minibatch, returns)
35:         end if
36:     end for
37:     if episode % 1000 == 0 then
38:         IF_detector.retrain(B.all_trusted_transitions())
39:     end if
40: end for
41: return θ
```

D.4  Shielded PPO Update with Trust Filtering

The PPO_update step implements the clipped surrogate objective:

L^{\text{CLIP}}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_t \left[ \min(r_t(\theta) \hat{A}_t, \,\text{clip}(r_t(\theta), 1-\epsilon, 1+\epsilon) \hat{A}_t) \right] + c_1 L^{\text{VF}}(\phi) + c_2 S[\pi_\theta](s_t),

where r_t(\theta) = \frac{\pi_\theta(a_t|s_t)}{\pi_{\text{old}}(a_t|s_t)}.  The
advantage \hat{A}_t is computed using GAE on the trusted minibatch.
Samples with trust score below \tau_{\min} are excluded before
advantage calculation, preventing them from affecting the gradient.

D.5  Interruptibility Algorithm with Rollback (Sketch)

```
Algorithm D.5: safe_interrupt_with_rollback(s_t, p_new, T_trial)
1:  verify p_new proofs
2:  pre_snapshot ← trilemma_metrics(s_t)
3:  s_sim ← shadow_simulate(p_new, s_t)
4:  if violations found then return REJECT
5:  broadcast migrate_proposal(p_new, s_sim, pre_snapshot)
6:  collect 2f+1 signatures → start_trial
7:  migrate to s_sim
8:  for epoch = 1 to T_trial do
9:      observe actual metrics
10:     if deviation(actual, s_sim) > Δ_tol then
11:         initiate_rollback(pre_snapshot)
12:         return ROLLBACK
13:     end if
14: end for
15: finalize_migration
```

D.6  Metric Computation Algorithms

Scalability: S(s) = (tx_confirmed_in_window / (window_time * τ_max)).
A moving average of transaction throughput is maintained.

Security: for each active protocol family, apply the corresponding
Phi_hat formula using current epoch’s validator set, fork tree, or
Snowball parameters.  Q = 1 - Phi_hat / Phi_0.

Decentralisation: maintain a histogram of block producers; recompute
entropy from the frequencies.

Energy: aggregate per‑validator measurements; sort; extract 95th
percentile.

All metrics are computed locally by validators and gossiped; a
consensus‑wide median (or trimmed mean) is taken to ensure uniformity.
Because the metrics are used in the reward, they must be deterministic
functions of the on‑chain state; the gossip step is therefore
supplemented by a commitment scheme and aggregated with BFT agreement.

